# Comments

@lede
Two comment forms, both stripped before any output. Brief refuses to put
comments inside text.
@end

## Line comments

A line comment starts with `//` and must occupy the entire line.

```brief
// This is a line comment.
// It will not appear in any output.
The next paragraph starts here.
```

Inline trailing comments do *not* exist. `paragraph text // comment` is a
literal paragraph; the `//` is text. This is intentional — restricting
comments to whole lines keeps the parser context-free.

## Block comments

Block comments use C-style `/* */` delimiters and may span multiple lines.

```brief
/*
This is a block comment.
It can span as many lines as it needs.
*/
```

Rules:

- Block comments cannot nest.
- The opening `/*` must appear at the start of a line, end of a line, or
  on its own line.
- The closing `*/` must appear at the start of a line, end of a line, or
  on its own line.
- A block comment in the middle of a paragraph is a compile error.

## What gets stripped

Comments are removed during the AST build phase. They do not appear in:

- HTML output.
- LLM output.
- JSON-AST output (the AST does not have comment nodes).

## When to use what

@dl
Line comments
: For one-shot annotations, TODO markers, and short context. The
  Markdown converter emits `// TODO[B-hole:...]` lines for constructs it
  could not translate cleanly.
Block comments
: For temporarily removing chunks of text — drafts, deprecated sections,
  scratch notes — without deleting the source.
@end

## Why no inline comments

Inline trailing comments would force the parser to track "is this `//` at
column 47 inside a code span, inside an emphasis, inside a shortcode body,
or inside plain text?" That kind of context-sensitivity is exactly what
Brief refuses elsewhere; allowing it for comments alone would be
inconsistent. Whole-line comments cost nothing and remove the question.
